<worksheet xml:id="ws-continuous-rv">
      <title>Random Variables of the Continuous Type (3.1)</title>
  <page>
    <p>
      Recall that a discrete random variable has a uniform distribution on
      <m>\{1,2,3,...,m\}</m> if every outcome in the space was equally likely.
      In that case the pmf was
      <md>
        f(x)=\frac{1}{m}.
      </md>
    </p>
<exercise>
  <introduction>
     <p>
      Suppose a random variable <m>X</m> is the outcome when a point is selected
      at random from the interval <m>[a,b]</m>, where 
      <m>-\infty \leq a \leq b \lt \infty</m>. If <m>x \in [a,b]</m> then if we
      tried to assign a probability <m>P(X=x)</m> as a fraction where the
      denominator was the number of possible outcomes in the interval
      <m>[a,b]</m> then we'd get that
      <m>f(x)=lim_{m\rightarrow \infty}\frac{1}{m}=0</m> for all <m>x</m>. So we
      instead lets look at the cdf (cumulative distribution function)
      <m>F(x)</m>. If the outcome is random, then what should <m>P(X\leq x)</m>
      be?
    </p>
     <p>
      <md>
        F(x)=\left\{ \begin{array}{ll}0, &amp; x \lt a,\\ \frac{x-a}{b-a}, &amp; a \leq x \lt b, \\ 1, &amp; b\leq x.\end{array} \right.
      </md>
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <task workspace = "0.5in">
    <statement>
        <p>
      Note that if we let  
      </p>
      <p>
      <m>
        f(x)=
      </m>
     </p>
    </statement>
  </task>
  <task workspace = "0.5in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        then we can write
      </p>
      <p>
      <m>
        F(x)=
      </m>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>
</exercise>
   
    <p>
      We call <m>f(x)</m> the
      <fillin characters = "75"/><!--{\bf probability density function pdf} of $X$.-->
    </p>

    <p>
      In the previous situation, we say <m>X</m> has a <fillin characters = "15"/> distribution and
      write: <fillin characters = "15"/><!--$X \sim U(a,b)$.\\-->
    </p>
</page>
<page>
<exercise workspace = "2in">
  <statement>
     <p>
      Let <m>X\sim U(0,4)</m>. Find <m>F(1)</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
</exercise>
   
<definition xml:id="def-pdf">
  <title>Probability Density Function (pdf)</title>
  
  
  <statement>
    <p>
      More generally, if the outcomes of an experiment come from an interval or
      union of intervals, <m>S</m>, instead of a discrete set, then the
      corresponding random variable is said to be of the
      <alert>continuous type</alert>. We define a probability density function
      (pdf) for a R.V. <m>X</m> of the continuous to be one such that
    </p>

    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>f(x)\geq 0\,\,\,\ \forall x \in S</m>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <m>{\int_S f(x)\,dx=1}</m>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            If <m>(a,b)\subseteq S</m> is an event, then
            <m>P(a \leq x \leq b)={\int_a^b f(x)\,dx}</m>
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </statement>
</definition>
    
<exercise workspace = ".5in">
  <statement>
    <p>
      For continuous distributions we define the <alert>cumulative distribution
      function (cdf)</alert> as <m>P(X \leq x)</m>:
    </p>
      <p>
      <m>
        F(x) =
      </m>
    </p>
  </statement>
</exercise>


    <p>
      NOTES:
    </p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\{a \leq x \leq b \}</m> is an event - for continuous data events
            are intervals or unions of intervals
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <m>f(x)</m> is NOT the probability that <m>x</m> occurs (and in
            particular can be greater than <m>1</m> for any given <m>x</m>).
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            We define <m>f(x)=0</m> for <m>x \not\in S</m> so that the
            <m>{\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}f(x)\,dx}</m> makes sense.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            By the fundamental theorem of calculus we get that
            <m>F'(x)=f(x)</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
</page>
<page>
  
<exercise>
